<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ercole I d'Este</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1431–1505</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ercole I d'Este, Duke of Ferrara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ercole I d'Este was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio from 1471 to 1505. A patron of the poet Boiardo, of music and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, he commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, a planned extension of Ferrara. In the War of Ferrara against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1482 to 1484 he lost the Polesine. His daughters Isabella and Beatrice married into the Gonzaga and Sforza families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este.jpg" alt="Ercole I d'Este" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosso Dossi / After Ercole de' Roberti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ercole_I_d%27Este.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
